  "story": "An aid convoy organized by a US-based NGO, Anera, was hit by an Israeli airstrike in Gaza, killing four local community members who had asked to serve as an escort for the convoy. The incident highlights the dangers faced by humanitarian workers delivering assistance in conflict zones, including threats from armed gangs, desperate civilians, and Israeli forces. The convoy was on its way to an Emirati-run hospital in Rafah when the lead vehicle was struck, resulting in the deaths of the four local guides. Anera partnered with a Dubai-based logistics company, Move One, to organize the mission, which aimed to provide medical supplies and fuel to the hospital. The NGO's statement on Friday characterized the local community members as having experience in security and community engagement with Move One, while earlier descriptions identified them as Move One employees. This event has sparked anger and concern over the safety of aid operations in Gaza. "
}

{
  "story": "Hundreds of ride-share drivers for Uber and Lyft in Nashville, Tennessee, have voted to begin a strike on Friday 30 August at Nashville international airport, warning of “ruined vacations” as the city prepares for a wave of tourists celebrating the Labor Day holiday weekend. The recently formed Tennessee Drivers Union is taking action to raise awareness of their demands for improvements to their pay and working conditions. The strike at the airport will be followed by a caravan of ride-share drivers through Nashville’s Lower Broadway entertainment district, a tourism hotspot. Tennessee recorded a record $30.6bn in tourism spending in 2023. The workers are pushing for improved compensation amid allegations that Uber and Lyft can take upwards of 60 to 80% of a ride fare. They are also calling for expanded access to bathrooms at the Nashville airport, limits on the use of scooters at night, a cap on the number of ride-share drivers permitted on the road and a ban on out-of-state drivers due to delays drivers can face in securing fares. “Most of the drivers in the Tennessee Drivers Union (TDU) work 12-hour days every day of the week doing rideshare. They depend on rideshare to be able to make a living, and Uber and Lyft provide their sole income,” the Tennessee Drivers Union said in a statement. “The drivers refuse to continue to let huge companies like Uber and Lyft profit off of its labor. They want Nashville to hear their struggle.”"
}

